<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <!--
    Spring Boot 3.5.16 — the latest published 3.x release, verified against
    the upstream release list before pinning (tag v3.5.16, published, not a
    prerelease).

    WHY 3.x AND NOT 4.x: 4.x is a new major line with breaking changes. This
    project's build had never been executed at the time the version was
    chosen, so pinning an unbuilt project to a brand-new major would have put
    two unverified risks in the same change. 3.5.x is the mature line and the
    APIs used here are stable in it.

    JAVA VERSION: Spring Boot 3.5.x builds on Java 17 as its baseline and
    supports 21. We pin 21 (the current LTS) deliberately — it satisfies the
    floor with room to spare. The Dockerfile base images, setup-java in the
    app-deploy workflow and the <release> below must all stay at 21: a drift
    between them fails either at compile time or with
    UnsupportedClassVersionError at runtime, and neither error names the
    version pin that actually caused it.
  -->
  <parent>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
    <version>3.5.16</version>
    <relativePath/>
  </parent>

  <groupId>com.udap</groupId>
  <artifactId>baseline-app</artifactId>
  <version>1.0.0</version>
  <name>baseline-app</name>
  <description>Landing page served from the eks-secure-baseline cluster</description>

  <properties>
    <java.version>21</java.version>
    <maven.compiler.release>21</maven.compiler.release>
    <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>

    <!--
      EMBEDDED TOMCAT SECURITY OVERRIDE — do not remove, do not lower.

      Spring Boot 3.5.16's dependency BOM manages embedded Tomcat at 10.1.55,
      which carries three CRITICAL CVEs (CVE-2026-65182, CVE-2026-65905,
      CVE-2026-68525). The image stage's Trivy scan gates on HIGH/CRITICAL and
      correctly refused to ship that image. All three are fixed in 10.1.58; we
      pin 10.1.60, the latest published 10.1.x, so the app is not one patch
      release behind the moment it ships.

      Overriding `tomcat.version` is the mechanism Spring Boot provides for
      exactly this: it upgrades the embedded container WITHOUT moving off the
      3.5.16 release train, so no other managed dependency shifts underneath
      us. The alternative — suppressing the findings in .trivyignore — would
      leave genuine remotely-exploitable CVEs in a container sitting behind an
      INTERNET-FACING ALB. That is not a trade we make.

      WHY THE EXPLICIT <dependencies> ENTRIES BELOW TOO: this property is the
      documented override, but if the BOM ever renames it the property would
      be silently ignored and the vulnerable 10.1.55 would return with no
      error — the build would go green and only Trivy would catch it. The
      explicit dependency declarations below make the upgrade independent of
      the property name, because a directly-declared version always wins over
      a BOM-managed one. Belt and braces, on purpose.

      Bumping this is a routine patch upgrade (embedded container only, no
      API surface used directly by this app). Verify the version is published
      on Maven Central before changing it.
    -->
    <tomcat.version>10.1.60</tomcat.version>
  </properties>

  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Actuator supplies the health endpoint the Kubernetes probes and the ALB
      target-group health check all call. Only /actuator/health is exposed;
      see application.yaml. Without this dependency the probes would have to
      hit '/', which returns 200 even when a dependency is broken.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      The three embedded Tomcat artifacts starter-web pulls in transitively,
      declared explicitly at the patched version. See the tomcat.version
      comment above: these exist so the security upgrade cannot be silently
      undone by a property rename in a future BOM. All three are published at
      10.1.60 on Maven Central (verified before pinning) — they are released
      together, so they must always move as a set.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.apache.tomcat.embed</groupId>
      <artifactId>tomcat-embed-core</artifactId>
      <version>${tomcat.version}</version>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.apache.tomcat.embed</groupId>
      <artifactId>tomcat-embed-el</artifactId>
      <version>${tomcat.version}</version>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.apache.tomcat.embed</groupId>
      <artifactId>tomcat-embed-websocket</artifactId>
      <version>${tomcat.version}</version>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
